# {{.ProviderShortName}} Provider

Use the Terrakube provider to manage [Terrakube](https://docs.terrakube.io) as code: organizations, projects,
workspaces and their variables, teams and access, VCS connections, modules, policies and notifications.

The provider talks to the Terrakube API, so it needs the API endpoint and a token. Each Terrakube server
version is supported by specific provider releases; see
[Supported Terrakube versions](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/terrakube-io/terrakube/latest/docs/guides/compatibility).

## Example Usage

{{ tffile "examples/provider/provider.tf" }}

```shell
export TERRAKUBE_ENDPOINT="https://terrakube-api.example.com"
export TERRAKUBE_TOKEN="..."
terraform plan
```

## Authentication

The provider needs two values. Set them with environment variables, or in the `provider` block.

| Setting | Environment variable | Value |
|---|---|---|
| `endpoint` | `TERRAKUBE_ENDPOINT` | The URL of the Terrakube **API**, not the UI. For example `https://terrakube-api.example.com`. |
| `token` | `TERRAKUBE_TOKEN` | A Terrakube API token. |

The provider can use two kinds of token:

- **Personal token.** Acts as the user who created it, with that user's permissions. Create one in the
  Terrakube UI under **Settings > Tokens**. See [API tokens](https://docs.terrakube.io/user-guide/organizations/api-tokens).
- **Team token.** Acts as a team, with only the permissions that team has in the organization. Use one
  for CI and other automation, so a pipeline doesn't run with a person's access. You can only create
  one for a team you belong to, in the Terrakube UI or with the `terrakube_team_token` resource.

Don't put the token in your configuration. If you need to set it in the `provider` block, pass it in
through a variable. With Terraform 1.10 or later, or OpenTofu 1.11 or later, make the variable
`ephemeral` so the token is never written to the plan or state:

```terraform
variable "terrakube_token" {
  type      = string
  sensitive = true
  ephemeral = true
}

provider "terrakube" {
  endpoint = "https://terrakube-api.example.com"
  token    = var.terrakube_token
}
```

`insecure_http_client` turns off TLS certificate checks. Use it only to test against a server with a
self-signed certificate.

## Secrets

Arguments that hold secrets, such as variable values, VCS client secrets and SSH private keys, have a
write-only version (`*_wo`) that is never stored in state. See
[Write-only arguments](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/terrakube-io/terrakube/latest/docs/guides/write-only-arguments).

To bring existing Terrakube objects under management, see
[Importing resources](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/terrakube-io/terrakube/latest/docs/guides/importing-resources).

